---
import LessonLayout  from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader  from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import CodeBlock     from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro';
import LessonCallout from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import DeepDive      from '@/components/DeepDive.astro';

const tokenizeExample = `
"hello" → [7, 4, 11, 11, 14]     # each character mapped to an integer id
`.trim();

const embedExample = `
token_id 7  → wte[7]  = [0.12, -0.44, 0.91, ...]   # what this token means
position 0  → wpe[0]  = [0.00,  0.03, -0.02, ...]   # where in the sequence it sits
input_vector = wte[token_id] + wpe[position]
`.trim();

const attentionSketch = `
for the token currently being processed:
  query  = "what am I looking for?"        (from this token)
  key    = "what do I contain?"            (from every cached token, incl. this one)
  value  = "what do I offer if you attend to me?"   (from every cached token)

score[i]  = dot(query, key[i]) / sqrt(head_dim)     # how relevant is token i?
weight[i] = softmax(score)[i]                       # normalize scores to sum to 1
output    = sum(weight[i] * value[i] for i in cached tokens)
`.trim();

const trainingLoss = `
loss = -log( probability the model assigned to the token that actually came next )

# training = repeatedly adjust every weight to make that probability higher
`.trim();

const kvCacheGrowth = `
pass 1: cache = [ K/V for "17" ]
pass 2: cache = [ K/V for "17", K/V for "×" ]
pass 3: cache = [ K/V for "17", K/V for "×", K/V for "20" ]
  ...
each new pass computes Q/K/V for only the ONE new token,
then attends that one query against every K/V already sitting in the cache.
`.trim();
---

<LessonLayout title="2.4 — How the Model Actually Works">

  <LessonHeader
    num="2.4"
    title="How the Model Actually Works"
    subtitle="Tokens, embeddings, attention, and training — grounded in Karpathy's microGPT, the smallest version that still teaches something true."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Lessons 2.1 and 2.2 treated "the layer stack" as a black box — a fixed pipeline that
      somehow lets a later pass "read" an earlier token. This lesson opens that box. Every
      piece below is a real, load-bearing part of how a transformer works, distilled from
      Andrej Karpathy's from-scratch microGPT implementation — nothing here is decorative.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">1. Tokenization — Text Becomes Integers</h2>

    <p>
      A model can't read letters. The first step of everything is a lookup table: every
      character (or, in production models, every sub-word chunk) gets assigned an integer id.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="tokenize.py" code={tokenizeExample} />

    <p>
      That's it — tokenization is bookkeeping, not intelligence. A small toy model might use
      ~27 possible tokens (letters + a stop token); production models use tens of thousands of
      sub-word chunks (byte-pair encoding), so common words are one token and rare words split
      into several.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">2. Embeddings — Integers Become Vectors</h2>

    <p>
      An integer id carries no meaning by itself — <code>7</code> isn't "more" or "less" than
      <code>4</code> in any useful sense. So each token id is looked up in a table of learned
      vectors (<code>wte</code>, "token embeddings") — during training, tokens that behave
      similarly end up with similar vectors. A second table (<code>wpe</code>, "position
      embeddings") encodes <em>where</em> in the sequence a token sits, since attention itself
      has no built-in sense of order. The two get added together to form what the model
      actually operates on.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="embed.py" code={embedExample} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">3. Attention — How a Pass Reads Earlier Tokens</h2>

    <p>
      This is the mechanism lesson 2.2 asserted without explaining. For the token currently
      being processed, the model computes three things from every token available to it (a
      <strong>query</strong>, a <strong>key</strong>, and a <strong>value</strong>), then asks:
      how relevant is each earlier token to what I need right now?
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="attention.py" code={attentionSketch} />

    <p>
      Read it plainly: <em>query</em> is "what am I looking for," <em>key</em> is "what do I
      contain" (offered by every token, including earlier ones), and the dot product between
      them is a relevance score — high when a key matches what the query wants. Those scores
      get normalized with softmax into weights that sum to 1, and the output is a weighted
      blend of every token's <em>value</em>. This is the literal mechanism behind "a later pass
      can read an earlier token" — not a metaphor, an actual weighted sum over real vectors.
    </p>

    <p>
      Real models don't do this once per pass — they do it in parallel across several
      <strong>heads</strong>, each learning to attend to different kinds of relationships (one
      head might track "what's the subject of this clause," another "what number was mentioned
      three steps back"), then concatenate and combine the results.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="The KV cache, precisely">
      <p>
        Lesson 2.2 said a new pass "reads the entire sequence so far" — true in effect, but
        mechanically imprecise. Real implementations cache each token's key and value the
        moment they're computed, so a new pass only computes Q/K/V for the <em>one new token</em>
        and attends it against the growing cache — it never recomputes attention for tokens
        already processed.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="kv-cache.py" code={kvCacheGrowth} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">4. Residuals and the Feedforward Block</h2>

    <p>
      Two more pieces per layer, briefly: a <strong>residual connection</strong> adds each
      block's input back onto its output, which is what makes stacking dozens of layers
      trainable at all (it gives gradients a direct path back through the network during
      training, instead of having to flow through every transformation). And a small
      <strong>feedforward network</strong> follows attention in every layer — if attention is
      "communication between token positions," the feedforward block is "computation within
      one position," done independently for every token.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">5. Training — Just: Raise the Right Probability</h2>

    <p>
      Everything above describes a model that already has learned weights. Training is what
      sets those weights, and the objective is simpler than it sounds:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="loss.py" code={trainingLoss} />

    <p>
      Feed the model real text, one position at a time, and check what probability it assigned
      to the token that actually came next. Adjust every weight — via backpropagation, the same
      chain-rule machinery regardless of model size — to make that probability slightly higher.
      Repeat billions of times. Nothing more exotic than that produces everything this whole
      lesson sequence has been built on.
    </p>

    <p>
      One more knob worth naming since it appears in every API you'll use:
      <strong>temperature</strong>. Before turning scores into probabilities, you can divide
      them by a temperature value first — near 0 makes the highest-scoring token dominate
      almost completely (near-deterministic output), higher values flatten the distribution
      out (more variety, more risk of a low-probability token getting picked).
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="Nothing here mentions reasoning">
      <p>
        Notice this whole lesson never needed the word "reasoning." Tokenization, embeddings,
        attention, training — none of it is CoT-specific. Chain-of-thought is a
        <em>behavior</em> that emerges from prompting (or, per lesson 2.1's newsletter research,
        from RL training) on top of this base mechanism — it's not a separate architectural
        component sitting somewhere in the stack.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

  </div>

  <DeepDive
    sources="karpathy-microgpt, ibm-ai-reasoning"
    tensions={[
      { a: 'Mechanistic transparency', b: 'Reasoning faithfulness' },
      { a: 'Toy-model clarity', b: 'Production-model scale' },
    ]}
    questions={[
      {
        q:      'We now know exactly how a token gets attended to (query/key/value dot products). Does that mean we can now trust that a written chain-of-thought step accurately reflects what the model actually computed?',
        hint:   'Think about what attention weights tell you versus what they don\'t.',
        answer: '<p>No — and this is the "faithfulness" gap the CoT-visualization research (lesson 2.1/2.2 research pass) explicitly flags as unresolved. Attention weights tell you which tokens influenced a computation, not whether the written-out reasoning text is an honest description of that computation. A model can write a clean-looking derivation while its actual attention patterns did something else entirely. Understanding the mechanism precisely does not, by itself, verify the narrative the model writes about itself.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'A toy model like microGPT has one layer and a 27-token vocabulary. What actually changes at production scale (GPT-4-class), and what stays exactly the same in kind?',
        hint:   'Separate "more of the same thing" from "a genuinely different mechanism."',
        answer: '<p>Stays the same in kind: tokenize → embed → attention + feedforward per layer → predict next token → sample → append → repeat. That loop is identical. What changes is scale and engineering: tens of thousands of sub-word tokens instead of characters, dozens to hundreds of layers instead of one, GPU-parallelized tensor math instead of scalar Python, additions like RoPE (a more sophisticated position encoding) and grouped-query attention (an efficiency trick for the KV cache), and a post-training stage (supervised fine-tuning + RLHF) that turns a raw next-token completer into something that behaves like a helpful chatbot rather than just continuing arbitrary text.</p>',
      },
    ]}
    pitfalls={[
      'Treating attention weights as a transparent, human-readable explanation of "why" the model answered a certain way — they are a real computational signal, not a plain-language justification.',
      'Assuming a bigger model has a fundamentally different generation mechanism than a toy one — it does not; it has more of the same fixed structure, plus post-training on top.',
      'Confusing embeddings with meaning in any human sense — a token vector is just coordinates the model found useful for predicting text well, not a definition.',
    ]}
  />

</LessonLayout>
